//? Post: cp ./build/README.org .

\image(assets/logo.png)

> mep

~An editor where everything is in place.~

mep is a modal, Vim-style code editor for Linux, written from the ground
up in C\+\+20 with embedded Lua. There is no UI toolkit, no browser engine
and no PDF library underneath it: the window, the renderer, the font
rasterizer, the regex engine, the terminal emulator and the readers for
every format it opens are all in this repository.

That buys the thing the name is about — one place to work. A C\+\+ file, a
shell, a PDF, a Jupyter notebook, a 3D scene and a live web page are all
just panes in the same window, driven by the same keys, scripted through
the same Lua API.

\image(assets/screenshot.png)

> Install

>> Nix (no clone needed)

```{sh, eval="no"}
nix run github:jordanschupbach/mep              # run it
nix profile install github:jordanschupbach/mep  # install it
```

>> From source

You need CMake ≥ 3.28, a C\+\+20 compiler, and — on the system — OpenGL,
X11 (with `Xtst` and `Xi`), ALSA and OpenSSL. The configure step fetches
Lua 5.4, so it needs network access the first time.

```{sh, eval="no"}
git clone https://github.com/jordanschupbach/mep
cd mep
cmake -S . -B build/native -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build/native -j
sudo cmake --install build/native      # optional; installs `mep`
```

Inside the repo, [just|https://github.com/casey/just] wraps the same thing: `just run` builds and
launches, `just build-native` only builds, `just clean` removes
`build/`. `nix develop` (or `direnv allow`) drops you in a shell with the
whole toolchain — compiler, cmake, just, deno, emscripten, language
servers and Tree-sitter grammars — already on `PATH`.

>> Platforms

Native mep targets *Linux/X11*, *macOS* (Cocoa/NSOpenGL) and *Windows*
(Win32/WGL). On macOS
you need Homebrew's `cmake`, `ninja`, `just`, `llvm` (Apple's toolchain
ships no `clang-scan-deps`, which CMake needs for this project's C\+\+20
modules) and `openssl@3`:

```{sh, eval="no"}
brew install cmake ninja just llvm openssl@3
just run
```

The justfile picks the Homebrew LLVM toolchain and the Ninja generator
automatically on macOS. Not yet ported there: audio (ALSA; silent
no-op), embedding other programs' windows (X11 reparenting), the
agent API's synthetic mouse/keyboard input (XTest), and Retina
rendering (the GL surface is 1x and upscaled for now).

>>> Windows

Needs Visual Studio 2022 (or the standalone Build Tools) with the
"Desktop development with C\+\+" workload, and CMake ≥ 3.28. Ninja is
required rather than the Visual Studio generator — this project's C\+\+20
modules need a generator that does module dependency scanning — and one
ships inside the VS install, so there is usually nothing extra to get.

From a repository checkout, with `just` (`winget install Casey.Just`):

```bat
just run
```

or without it:

```bat
scripts\windows-build.bat
.\build\win\mep.exe
```

`just run` is the same two steps: the `build-native` and `run` recipes
have `[windows]` variants that build into `build\win` through the batch
script and launch `build\win\mep.exe`, and the justfile sets
`windows-shell` to `cmd.exe` since just's default `sh` does not exist
here. Only those recipes (plus `clean`) are ported — `just test`,
`bench`, `help` and the `lint` family are still bash, and need WSL or
Git Bash.

That script finds the VS install with `vswhere`, runs `vcvars64.bat` to
put `cl.exe` and the SDK on `PATH`, puts the bundled Ninja on `PATH` if
there is no system one, then configures and builds into `build\win`. To
drive CMake yourself, run `vcvars64.bat` first and then the ordinary
`cmake -S . -B build/win -G Ninja` — nothing about the configuration is
specific to the script. (`vcvars64.bat` itself prints a stray
`"vswhere.exe" is not recognized` on some installs; it is harmless
noise from inside Microsoft's own script, not a failure.)

Editing, every document type, the renderer, Tree-sitter highlighting,
Lua scripting and the mouse/keyboard input path all work. Not yet
ported, and *compiled out* rather than failing at runtime:

- *Everything that spawns a child process.* The `:terminal` pane, LSP
  servers, `:!` commands, the Jupyter/notebook kernels and the YouTube
  pane's `yt-dlp`/`ffmpeg` all go through `job.cpp`, which is
  `fork`/`forkpty` on POSIX. Windows has no `fork`; the equivalent is
  `CreateProcess` plus ConPTY (`CreatePseudoConsole`) for the terminal.
  This is the single largest remaining piece.
- *Sockets.* The agent RPC API, the HTTP client/server, the LSP
  transport and the collaboration relay are all behind the same
  `!defined(_WIN32)` guard the wasm build already used. Winsock is close
  enough to BSD sockets that this is mostly mechanical.
- *Audio* — a silent no-op, as on macOS. WASAPI is the Windows
  counterpart of the Linux ALSA backend.
- *Generators and `async`/`await` in the embedded JS engine*, which run
  each body on its own stack via `ucontext`. Windows fibers map onto
  that directly; see `js_engine.cpp`'s note.
- *Embedding other programs' windows* (`exec-gui` blocks) and the agent
  API's synthetic input — X11-specific, the same gap macOS has.
- *Collaboration* needs OpenSSL for the WebSocket handshake. If CMake
  does not find one the build substitutes a stub and says so; `:collab`
  then reports a connection failure. Point `-DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR` at an
  install (vcpkg's works) to enable it.

One known rough edge: the window does not redraw while a resize is being
dragged, because Windows runs its own modal message loop for the
duration and mep's frame loop does not get to run. The contents snap
back on release.

There is also a WebAssembly build
(`just build-web`) that runs in a browser, and `just run-wasm` opens it
in a desktop window via Deno + webview; the wasm build has no host
filesystem, so file I/O there goes through a small local bridge
([details|file:docs/architecture.org]).

> Getting started

```{sh, eval="no"}
mep                 # opens the dashboard on the current directory
mep path/to/file    # opens a file
```

- The leader key is `Space`. Press it and wait: a which-key popup lists
  every group and binding. `<leader>hk` searches all of them.
- `<leader>hh` (or `:MepHelp`) opens the built-in help.
- `<leader>ff` file tree, `<leader>/` find in buffer, `<leader>pr`
  project-wide grep, `<leader>bb` buffer picker, `<leader><CR>` a
  terminal for this tab.
- Vim's own `:` commands mostly work as you'd expect — `:w`, `:e`,
  `:split`, `:%s/pat/repl/g`, `:g/pat/d`.

The full reference is [docs/keybindings.org|file:docs/keybindings.org].

> What's in the box

\image(assets/gallery.png)

*Editing.* Normal / Insert / Replace / Visual (charwise, linewise,
blockwise) / Command-line modes. Counted motions and operators, text
objects, registers (the unnamed register *is* the system clipboard),
marks, the jump list, `.`\-repeat, macros, per-buffer undo. Regex search
and `:s`/`:g`, with backreferences. A flash.nvim-style quick jump (`s`)
that labels matches across every visible pane. Tree-sitter highlighting,
with \~49 further languages a |just fetch-grammars| away.

*Windows.* Splits and tabs with directional navigation (`Ctrl-W hjkl`,
`mod1+hjkl`), per-pane buffer tabs, pane zoom and maximize. Projects
contain workspaces (backed by git worktrees) contain tabs contain panes,
and each project's layout is saved and restored.

*IDE.* LSP — completion, hover, definition, references, implementation,
rename, code actions, diagnostics, formatting. DAP debugging. Snippets,
spell check, a task runner that finds your `just`/`make`/`ninja`/`cmake`
setup. GitHub Copilot inline completions ([setup|file:docs/copilot.org]).

*Git.* Gutter signs against any base ref, hunk preview, staging and
navigation, and a five-view panel: status, log, graph, branches, stash.

*Sidebars.* File tree, symbols, structure outline, git, todo, tests,
notifications and AI agents — dockable to either edge, stacked and
resizable, and each one pops out into a large preview float with
`mod1+m`.

*Beyond text.* Every one of these opens as a pane, from mep's own code:

| Open this | And you get |
| --- | --- |
| `.pdf` | a PDF viewer with real text extraction, outlines and links |
| `.html` (or `:Browse <url>`) | a browser pane with mep's own HTML/CSS/JS engine; `:Serve` hosts a directory ([more|file:docs/web.org]) |
| `.png`, `.jpg`, … | an image viewer, and `e` enters a layer-based editor with procedural fills ([more|file:IMAGE_EDITOR.md]) |
| `.obj`, `.gltf`, `.glb`, `.iqm`, `.vox`, `.m3d` | a 3D modeler: mesh editing, materials, lights, animation, rendering (`.blend` too, via a local Blender) |
| `.ipynb` | a Jupyter notebook with per-cell kernels, run without `jupyter_client` ([more|file:docs/notebooks.org]) |
| `.org` | agenda, babel, roam, capture, clock, drill, LaTeX and image preview, export, plus `:Gantt` and `:Kanban` views |
| `.docx`, `.odt`, `.xlsx`, `.ods`, `.csv` | office documents and spreadsheets, with formulas |
| `.mov` | a video player |
| `:terminal` | a real terminal emulator |

*Collaboration and agents.* CRDT-based shared editing over a
self-hosted relay ([docs|file:docs/collaboration.org]). An agent-control socket, and a `mep-mcp`
MCP server on top of it, let an AI agent drive the editor directly —
buffers, panes, workspaces, the image editor and the 3D modeler — while
you watch ([API|file:MEP_AGENT_API.md]).

*Learning games.* An org file doubles as a flashcard deck, and 24 game
modes deal sessions from it ([docs|file:docs/learn-games.org]).

> Configuration

mep reads `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mep/init.lua` (or `~/.config/mep/init.lua`)
at startup. It is ordinary Lua against the same `mep.*` API the editor's
own built-in configuration uses.

```{lua, eval="no"}
-- One of 62 built-in themes; :MepTheme picks one interactively.
mep.cmd('colorscheme gruvbox-dark')

-- A command and a leader binding of your own.
mep.command('Hello', function() mep.notify('hello from init.lua') end)
mep.leader_map('xh', 'Say hello', function() mep.cmd('Hello') end)

-- Rebind the mod1 modifier, and turn Copilot off.
mep.set_mod1('super')
mep.copilot_enabled = false
```

`:lua <code>` evaluates a line, `:source <file>` loads a script. The
whole surface is in [docs/lua-api.org|file:docs/lua-api.org].

