> Development

```{sh, eval="no"}
just run                # build and launch
just build-native-dev   # -O0 build in build/native-dev, for fast iteration
just test               # windowless unit tests
just test-gui           # the tests that drive a real window
just bench              # performance suite, results in bench_results/
just lint               # clang-tidy + cppcheck + include-what-you-use
just readme             # build README.mepml's exports, refresh README.org
```

Tests and benchmarks are `EXCLUDE_FROM_ALL`, so an ordinary build
produces only `mep`, `mep-mcp`, `mep-collabd`, `mep-org-lsp`,
`mep-r-lsp`, `mep-python-lsp`, `mep-cpp-lsp`, `mep-c-lsp` and
`mep-maxima-lsp`. Start at
[docs/architecture.org|file:docs/architecture.org] for the layout of the source tree.

This README is written in mepml, mep's own document format: `README.mepml`
is the source, and `just readme` exports it to the `README.org` GitHub
shows. Edit the `.mepml`, not the `.org`.

Like every mepml document, it is built into a directory rather than beside
its source: `just readme` (which is `mep-mepml build README.mepml org md
html`) writes `build/README.org`, `build/README.md` and `build/README.html`,
and the document's own post-build command then copies the `.org` back to
the top of the repository:

```{mepml, eval="no"}
//? Post: cp ./build/README.org .
```

`//? Build: dir` picks another directory (`.` is the document's own), and
`//? Post:` runs any shell command after an export -- from the editor
(the play button, `<leader>ke`, `:MepmlExport`) and from the command line
alike -- with `MEP_SOURCE`, `MEP_BUILD` and `MEP_OUT` in its environment.
See [Exporting and importing|file:help/mepml.org] in the mepml guide.

